# Game state access

- Code that is not handed a `GameState` reads it through `CurrentGameState()` (`include/game/CurrentGameState.h`).
- A function or method that only reads the game takes no `GameState` parameter. A `GameState&` parameter means the function may write.
- A free function that needs ruleset data it is not handed reads `CurrentGameState().GetGameData()`. Classes take the ruleset pieces they use in their constructors.
- Code that runs outside a game (loading, validators, world generation) takes `GameDataContext` explicitly.
- Game objects (factions, bases, units and their parts) never hold a `GameState`.
- Nothing crosses game states by pointer: name factions, bases and units by id. Only the thread that installed a game state writes to it.
- Constructors never install a `GameStateScope`. `Engine`, tests and worker threads do.
